Yes. And this makes Afterglow² less metaphorical than it first sounds.

A visual image can leave a literal visual afterimage due to adaptation in the visual system. Separately, a remembered or imagined image can leave cognitive consequences: priming associations, altering attention, changing what becomes salient next, evoking another image, and influencing later interpretation.

The Pantheon abstraction is broader:

X_0
\xrightarrow{\text{experience}}
A_1

where A_1 is the first residual effect.

But that residual becomes a new input:

A_1\xrightarrow{T_1}A_2
\xrightarrow{T_2}A_3
\xrightarrow{T_3}\cdots

So:

\boxed{\text{Afterglow}^n=T_n(T_{n-1}(\cdots T_1(X)))}

The interesting thing is that these aren’t necessarily fading photocopies. Each generation can change medium.

An image might produce:

\text{image}
\rightarrow
\text{felt impression}
\rightarrow
\text{word}
\rightarrow
\text{metaphor}
\rightarrow
\text{operator}
\rightarrow
\text{code}
\rightarrow
\text{new image}.

That’s more like a transduction cascade than ordinary memory.

And now ROM becomes richer. The valuable memory isn’t necessarily the original X. Sometimes the valuable object is an A_3 or A_7 that didn’t exist at encoding time:

\boxed{
ROM(X)=
\sum_{n=1}^{N}
\gamma^n U(A_n)
}

Conceptually, \gamma discounts increasingly remote descendants, while U(A_n) measures useful downstream effects. But Pantheon should also track surprise jackpots where a remote afterglow has disproportionate utility.

That gives us three different phenomena worth separating:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{Recall} &: X\rightarrow \hat X\\
\text{Afterglow} &: X\rightarrow A_1\\
\text{Afterglow}^n &: X\rightarrow A_1\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow A_n
\end{aligned}}

And Nitinol gives us a fourth:

\boxed{
\text{Restoration}: X\rightarrow X'\rightarrow\hat X
}

Now combine them:

X
\rightarrow
X'
\rightarrow
A_1
\rightarrow
A_2
\rightarrow
\hat X.

The system returns to the old idea after having traveled through its descendants.

Therefore \hat X may not equal X:

\boxed{\hat X=X+\Delta_{\text{journey}}}

That’s recursive reinterpretation in a very compact form.

And there’s a particularly useful experiment hiding here for Vacuum Spectroscopy: don’t only vacuum parts of the original stimulus. Vacuum an afterglow.

Remove the intermediate metaphor, image, category, or memory that normally connects X to A_2, and see whether the system can route around it:

X\rightarrow A_1\rightarrow
\boxed{\varnothing}
\rightarrow A_3?

If it can, there are redundant semantic routes.

If it cannot, we’ve discovered a load-bearing memory junction.

If it invents a new route:

X\rightarrow A_1\rightarrow B_2\rightarrow A_3,

we’ve watched Tracklayer operate under ablation.

That gives Afterglow Spectroscopy:

Perturb different generations of a memory/transformation cascade and measure which later meanings survive, disappear, mutate, or regenerate.

And that gives ROM a better unit of analysis. Don’t ask merely:

“Did we remember this?”

Ask:

“How many useful futures did remembering this make reachable?”

\boxed{
ROM(m)
\propto
\Delta |\text{reachable useful states}|
}

That’s a much closer description of why some tiny Pantheon fossils keep paying dividends long after their original context is gone.

The memory isn’t merely preserved.

It keeps having children.